# Track E1 KNOWN GAP (FILED, LLVM LANE, MEMORY SAFETY) — `g.0 = v` on
# `Guard[(int, int)]` SIGSEGVs on LLVM.
#
# CATEGORY: tuple-field write-through through a Guard receiver. The tuple-field
# assign path (Track D's territory in the round handover) does NOT project
# through `emit_guard_get_ptr`, so the destination pointer computed for `g.0`
# is bogus and the LLVM backend segfaults on the store.
#
# FIX DIRECTION (per /tmp/scoutE_report_final.md follow-up #2): the tuple-field
# write-through path needs to route through the Guard's `emit_guard_get_ptr`
# projection, analogous to how struct-field assign already handles GuardAccept.
# May be closed under a future Track-D-adjacent fix that generalizes the guard
# write-place projection.
#
# INTENDED (after fix): prints "(42, 2)" — the write lands, no crash.
# E1 pre-fix behavior (verified 2026-07-27):
#   - C lane:    C-compile ERROR (`tests/fixtures/.../*.c: In function 'main':`
#                `Guard__Tuple__int64_t__int64_t has no member named 'owner'`).
#   - LLVM lane: build OK, run SIGSEGVs (exit 139).
# Both are broken; the LLVM shape is what the scout named. UNCHANGED by this
# Track (E1 closes the terminal-fallthrough fabrication class only).
void main():
    Mutex[(int, int)] m = Mutex[(int, int)]((1, 2))
    Guard[(int, int)] g = m.lock()
    g.0 = 42
    print(g.get())
